    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which anatomical concept best models the collapsible nature of the human upper airway during sleep?', '["The Bernoulli principle", "The Starling resistor model", "Poiseuille''s law", "Boyle''s law", "The Venturi effect"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Starling resistor model describes a collapsible tube passing through a chamber with varying surrounding pressures. The human upper airway behaves like this, collapsing when surrounding tissue pressure (or upstream nasal resistance) exceeds the intraluminal pressure holding it open.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'According to the concept of Critical Closing Pressure (Pcrit), what does a POSITIVE Pcrit indicate about a patient''s upper airway?', '["The airway is very stiff and unlikely to collapse", "The airway is highly collapsible and requires continuous positive pressure (CPAP) just to stay open", "The patient has central sleep apnea", "The patient has a normal, healthy airway", "The patient has laryngeal cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A normal, healthy airway has a highly negative Pcrit (e.g., -10 cm H2O), meaning it requires significant suction to collapse. A positive Pcrit (e.g., +2 cm H2O) means the airway will passively collapse at atmospheric pressure unless positive pressure is applied to stent it open.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which muscle is considered the primary dilator of the upper airway, responsible for pulling the tongue forward and preventing retrolingual collapse?', '["Palatoglossus", "Styloglossus", "Hyoglossus", "Genioglossus", "Superior pharyngeal constrictor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The genioglossus muscle constitutes the bulk of the tongue. Its contraction pulls the tongue base anteriorly. During sleep, reduced tone in this muscle is a major driver of Obstructive Sleep Apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cranial nerve provides the motor innervation to the genioglossus muscle?', '["Trigeminal (CN V)", "Facial (CN VII)", "Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)", "Vagus (CN X)", "Hypoglossal (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) innervates the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue, including the genioglossus. This is the anatomical basis for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation (Inspire) therapy for OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During which stage of sleep are apneic events typically the most frequent, severe, and associated with the deepest oxygen desaturations?', '["Stage N1 (light sleep)", "Stage N2", "Stage N3 (slow-wave sleep)", "REM (Rapid Eye Movement) sleep", "Wakefulness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'REM sleep is characterized by profound skeletal muscle atonia (paralysis), which includes the pharyngeal dilator muscles (like the genioglossus). This loss of protective muscle tone makes the airway highly vulnerable to collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What happens to the ventilatory response to hypercapnia (high CO2) and hypoxia during normal sleep, compared to wakefulness?', '["It becomes much more sensitive", "It remains completely unchanged", "It becomes blunted (less sensitive)", "The body stops sensing CO2 entirely", "It triggers immediate awakening at normal CO2 levels"]'::jsonb, 2, 'During sleep, the central chemoreceptors become less sensitive to CO2, allowing the PaCO2 to rise slightly (2-8 mmHg). This blunted response contributes to the prolongation of apneic events before an arousal is triggered.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does obesity physically alter the upper airway to increase the risk of OSA?', '["By causing hypertrophy of the thyroid gland", "By depositing fat directly into the laryngeal cartilages", "By depositing fat in the lateral pharyngeal walls and tongue base, narrowing the airway lumen and increasing the critical closing pressure (Pcrit)", "By destroying the hypoglossal nerve", "By shrinking the size of the tonsils"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fat deposition in the parapharyngeal fat pads and within the genioglossus muscle itself physically crowds the pharyngeal airway, making it smaller and much more prone to collapse under negative inspiratory pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Aside from direct airway narrowing, how does central (truncal) obesity contribute pathophysiologically to the severity of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["It increases the strength of the diaphragm", "It decreases functional residual capacity (FRC), leading to a lower oxygen reservoir and more rapid oxygen desaturation during an apnea", "It increases lung compliance", "It paralyzes the vocal cords", "It causes central sleep apnea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Truncal obesity compresses the chest wall and diaphragm, significantly reducing FRC. With a smaller reservoir of oxygen in the lungs, the patient''s oxygen saturation plummets much faster when they stop breathing.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following craniofacial abnormalities is a major, independent risk factor for the development of severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Macrostomia (large mouth)", "Micrognathia/Retrognathia (small, recessed lower jaw)", "Prominent cheekbones", "Large ears", "A deviated nasal septum alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A small or set-back mandible forces the base of the tongue backward into the pharyngeal airway, severely narrowing the retrolingual space even in non-obese individuals.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Müller Maneuver" attempting to simulate in a clinical setting?', '["A massive sneeze", "The positive pressure of CPAP", "The negative intrathoracic pressure generated during an inspiratory effort against an obstructed airway (simulating an apneic event)", "The pressure in the middle ear", "The swallowing reflex"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Müller maneuver (forced inspiration against a closed mouth and nose) generates negative pharyngeal pressure. ENT surgeons use it during awake endoscopy to observe which parts of the floppy airway collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During an obstructive apneic event, the patient continues to make forceful inspiratory efforts against a closed airway. What cardiovascular consequence does this massive negative intrathoracic pressure cause?', '["It increases cardiac output", "It causes massive venous pooling in the legs", "It increases venous return to the right heart, shifting the interventricular septum to the left, which decreases left ventricular stroke volume and cardiac output", "It cures hypertension", "It slows the heart rate permanently"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The huge negative pressure acts like a vacuum, sucking blood into the right heart. The engorged right ventricle pushes the septum into the left ventricle, impairing left heart filling and dropping cardiac output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common anatomical site of upper airway obstruction in adult patients with OSA?', '["The nasal cavity only", "The true vocal folds", "The trachea", "The retropalatal (oropharynx) and retrolingual (hypopharynx) regions, often at multiple levels simultaneously", "The bronchi"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Adult OSA is rarely a single-site issue. Most adults have multi-level collapse involving the soft palate/uvula (retropalatal) AND the base of tongue/epiglottis (retrolingual).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which anatomical feature defines the "retropalatal" airway?', '["The space behind the nasal bones", "The space behind the soft palate and uvula, bounded laterally by the tonsillar pillars", "The space behind the base of the tongue", "The space below the vocal cords", "The space inside the larynx"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The retropalatal space is a highly collapsible segment. Enlarged tonsils, a long thick uvula, or a webbed soft palate can easily obstruct this area when muscle tone drops during sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old male has severe nasal septal deviation and inferior turbinate hypertrophy. How does isolated nasal obstruction contribute to Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["It directly blocks the trachea", "It causes central sleep apnea", "It requires the patient to generate much higher negative inspiratory pressure downstream to pull air through the nose, which sucks the floppy pharyngeal walls inward, causing collapse", "It paralyzes the tongue", "It has no effect on sleep apnea"]'::jsonb, 2, 'High upstream (nasal) resistance means the lungs must pull harder (more negative pressure). This vacuum effect collapses the downstream pharynx (the Starling resistor model). Furthermore, it forces mouth breathing, which pushes the jaw and tongue backward.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does mouth breathing, compared to nasal breathing, physically worsen upper airway collapsibility?', '["Mouth breathing increases tongue muscle tone", "Mouth breathing rotates the mandible backward and inferiorly, which pushes the base of the tongue directly into the posterior pharyngeal airway", "Mouth breathing shrinks the tonsils", "Mouth breathing bypasses the lungs", "Mouth breathing cools the airway, making it stiff"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Opening the mouth hinges the jaw backward. Since the genioglossus muscle is attached to the mandible, the tongue falls backward with it, crowding the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary pathophysiologic difference between Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA) and Central Sleep Apnea (CSA)?', '["OSA only occurs in children; CSA only in adults", "OSA is caused by obesity; CSA is caused by thinness", "In OSA, there is an anatomical blockage but respiratory effort continues; in CSA, there is no blockage, but the brain simply stops sending the signal to breathe", "OSA causes hypercapnia; CSA causes hypocapnia", "There is no difference"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In OSA, you see the chest and abdomen heaving against a closed throat. In CSA (often due to heart failure or stroke), the chest and abdomen are completely still during the apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following neck circumference measurements is a strong, independent clinical predictor of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Greater than 12 inches in a man", "Greater than 17 inches (43 cm) in a man, or greater than 16 inches (40 cm) in a woman", "Less than 15 inches in a man", "Greater than 10 inches in a woman", "Neck size does not correlate with OSA"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A thick neck indicates significant parapharyngeal fat deposition, which correlates strongly with a narrow, highly collapsible upper airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which demographic is generally considered at the highest risk for developing Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Young, thin females", "Pre-menopausal women", "Middle-aged, obese males", "Children with small tonsils", "Elderly underweight males"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Middle-aged, obese men are the classic demographic. However, post-menopausal women quickly "catch up" in risk, likely due to the loss of the protective effects of estrogen and progesterone on muscle tone and fat distribution.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the hormone Progesterone affect respiration, providing a protective effect against sleep apnea in pre-menopausal women?', '["It acts as a strong central respiratory stimulant", "It paralyzes the diaphragm", "It makes the airway cartilage stiffer", "It shrinks the tonsils", "It blocks the hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Progesterone stimulates the respiratory drive in the brainstem. This is why OSA prevalence in women spikes dramatically after menopause when progesterone levels drop.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which congenital syndrome, characterized by mandibular hypoplasia, glossoptosis, and cleft palate, is notoriously associated with severe neonatal obstructive sleep apnea?', '["Down Syndrome", "Pierre Robin Sequence", "Turner Syndrome", "Marfan Syndrome", "Cystic Fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In Pierre Robin sequence, the micrognathia forces the tongue to fall backward (glossoptosis), obstructing the airway. These infants often require prone positioning, surgical lip-tongue adhesion, or mandibular distraction osteogenesis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why are children with Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21) at a highly elevated risk for Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["They have very large, stiff mandibles", "They invariably have central nervous system failure", "They have macroglossia (large tongue), midface hypoplasia, and generalized generalized hypotonia (low muscle tone)", "They do not have tonsils", "They have hyperactive pharyngeal reflexes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The combination of a small midface/jaw, a relatively large tongue, and weak pharyngeal muscle tone makes their airway extremely prone to collapse. Over 50% of children with Down Syndrome have OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the definition of an "Apnea"?', '["A 10% reduction in airflow", "A complete (or near complete, >90%) cessation of airflow for at least 10 seconds", "A snore that wakes the patient up", "Breathing too fast", "A pause in breathing for 2 seconds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An apnea is defined as a drop in peak signal excursion by ≥90% of pre-event baseline using an oronasal thermal sensor (or alternative), lasting for at least 10 seconds.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the definition of a "Hypopnea" in adults (according to standard AASM criteria)?', '["A complete cessation of breathing", "A >30% reduction in airflow lasting at least 10 seconds, associated with either a ≥3% oxygen desaturation OR an EEG arousal", "Any snore lasting 10 seconds", "A >50% reduction in airflow without desaturation", "A drop in heart rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hypopneas represent partial airway obstruction. To be clinically counted, the partial obstruction must cause a physiologic consequence: either dropping the blood oxygen or waking the brain up (arousal).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Respiratory Effort-Related Arousal" (RERA)?', '["A massive drop in oxygen without waking up", "A sequence of breaths characterized by increasing respiratory effort (flattening of the inspiratory flow curve) leading to an arousal from sleep, but which does not meet criteria for an apnea or hypopnea", "A central apnea", "A dream that causes sweating", "A completely normal breath"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RERAs represent very mild obstructions that don''t drop oxygen, but force the patient to work so hard to breathe that their brain wakes up to open the airway, fragmenting their sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What specific metric is calculated by combining the number of Apneas and the number of Hypopneas per hour of sleep?', '["The Respiratory Disturbance Index (RDI)", "The Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI)", "The Oxygen Desaturation Index (ODI)", "The Epworth Score", "The Mallampati Score"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The AHI is the primary metric used to diagnose and grade the severity of Obstructive Sleep Apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the Respiratory Disturbance Index (RDI) differ from the Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI)?', '["RDI only counts central apneas", "AHI is used in adults, RDI is used in children", "RDI = Apneas + Hypopneas + RERAs per hour of sleep; therefore, RDI is always equal to or higher than AHI", "RDI measures oxygen, AHI measures heart rate", "They are exactly the same thing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because the RDI includes RERAs (which are subtle events that fragment sleep but don''t drop oxygen), it captures a broader picture of sleep fragmentation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What pathophysiological mechanism links untreated Obstructive Sleep Apnea to the development of systemic hypertension?', '["OSA causes kidney failure", "Repeated hypoxic events and arousals trigger massive surges in sympathetic nervous system activity (catecholamines), which eventually resets the baseline blood pressure higher", "OSA causes excessive fluid drinking", "OSA destroys the vagus nerve", "OSA causes liver cirrhosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The recurrent "fight or flight" response triggered every time the patient chokes during sleep causes chronic sympathetic overactivity, leading to resistant hypertension, especially non-dipping nighttime hypertension.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cardiac arrhythmia is notoriously associated with and frequently triggered by severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Ventricular fibrillation", "Atrial Fibrillation (A-Fib)", "Third-degree heart block", "Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome", "Sinus tachycardia only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The massive negative intrathoracic pressure stretches the atria, while hypoxia and sympathetic surges alter cardiac conduction. Untreated OSA makes A-fib very difficult to control medically or with ablation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the effect of alcohol consumption before bedtime on a patient with mild Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["It acts as a stimulant and prevents apneas", "It selectively tightens the pharyngeal muscles, curing OSA", "It acts as a muscle relaxant, worsening pharyngeal collapse, and it blunts the brain''s arousal response to hypoxia, making the apneas significantly longer and more severe", "It has absolutely no effect on respiration", "It converts OSA to Central Sleep Apnea"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Alcohol is devastating for OSA patients. It makes the floppy airway even floppier and prevents the brain from waking up quickly when the patient starts suffocating.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which class of medications is notoriously dangerous for patients with severe, untreated Obstructive Sleep Apnea, particularly in the post-operative setting?', '["Antibiotics", "Opioids and Benzodiazepines", "Proton pump inhibitors", "Statins", "Beta-blockers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Opioids and benzodiazepines profoundly depress the central respiratory drive and relax pharyngeal muscles. In an OSA patient, this can lead to catastrophic, fatal airway obstruction and respiratory arrest in the recovery room.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA exhibits daytime hypercapnia (elevated PaCO2) and right heart failure (cor pulmonale) while awake. What is this specific advanced clinical syndrome called?', '["Pickwickian syndrome (Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome)", "Cushing''s syndrome", "Metabolic syndrome", "Marfan syndrome", "Down syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (OHS) occurs when severe obesity and OSA cause a blunted central respiratory drive even while awake, leading to chronic CO2 retention, pulmonary hypertension, and right heart failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What happens to the architectural stages of sleep in a patient with severe, untreated Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["They get too much REM sleep", "They spend 90% of the night in Stage N3 (deep sleep)", "Because of constant arousals, they are deprived of Stage N3 (deep slow-wave sleep) and REM sleep, spending most of the night bouncing between light sleep (N1) and wakefulness", "Their sleep architecture is perfectly normal", "They do not sleep at all"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The constant choking wakes the brain up just enough to open the airway (an arousal). This prevents the brain from ever settling down into the restorative deep stages of sleep, causing profound daytime fatigue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the context of upper airway anatomy, what is the "velopharynx"?', '["The airway behind the base of the tongue", "The airway extending from the hard palate to the tip of the uvula (the retropalatal airway)", "The airway below the epiglottis", "The nasal cavity", "The trachea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The velopharynx is the uppermost part of the collapsible pharynx. The soft palate (velum) forms its anterior wall. It is the most common single site of obstruction in OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the context of upper airway anatomy, what comprises the boundaries of the "oropharynx"?', '["From the tip of the nose to the hard palate", "From the hard palate down to the level of the hyoid bone, including the soft palate and the base of the tongue", "From the vocal cords to the trachea", "From the epiglottis to the cricoid", "The middle ear space"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oropharynx contains two major collapsible zones: the retropalatal space (behind the soft palate) and the retrolingual space (behind the base of the tongue).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which pharyngeal muscle acts like a sling to elevate and pull the soft palate forward, helping to keep the velopharynx open?', '["Tensor veli palatini", "Levator veli palatini", "Palatopharyngeus", "Palatoglossus", "Uvular muscle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tensor veli palatini tenses the soft palate, pulling it anteriorly away from the posterior pharyngeal wall. The levator elevates it backward (to close the nasopharynx during swallowing).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "pharyngeal critical pressure" (Pcrit) of a patient with severe OSA typically like, compared to a normal person?', '["Highly negative (e.g., -15 cm H2O)", "Mildly negative (e.g., -2 cm H2O)", "Positive (e.g., +4 cm H2O), meaning the airway collapses even before pressure drops below atmospheric", "Exactly zero", "Pcrit cannot be measured"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A positive Pcrit means the structural forces collapsing the airway (fat, floppy tissue) are stronger than the structural forces holding it open, even at rest. The airway will collapse unless positive pressure (CPAP) inflates it.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which mechanical property of the pharyngeal mucosa is often altered in chronic snorers and OSA patients, making the airway more likely to stick together once it collapses?', '["Increased cartilage formation", "Increased surface tension (the mucosa becomes dry, sticky, and lacks normal surfactant-like lubricants due to chronic vibration trauma)", "Decreased surface tension", "Complete loss of mucosal epithelium", "Formation of bone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Chronic snoring causes vibration trauma, leading to edema and altered mucosal secretions. The sticky mucosa requires a much higher "opening pressure" to pry it apart once it collapses.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the supine position (sleeping on the back) specifically worsen Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["It decreases blood flow to the brain", "Gravity pulls the soft palate, uvula, and the base of the tongue directly posteriorly against the posterior pharyngeal wall, physically occluding the airway", "It causes acid reflux", "It stimulates the vagus nerve", "It paralyzed the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Positional OSA is very common. Gravity is the enemy when supine. Many patients have severe OSA on their backs but mild or no OSA when sleeping on their sides.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "sleep state misperception"?', '["Dreaming about sleeping", "When a patient with severe OSA reports sleeping for 8 hours, but PSG shows they were actually awake or in Stage N1 for 90% of the night due to hundreds of micro-arousals", "When a patient thinks they are awake but they are asleep", "A side effect of CPAP", "A type of narcolepsy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'OSA patients rarely remember the hundreds of times they "wake up" (arouse) to breathe. They perceive they slept all night, but wonder why they are exhausted the next day.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a known metabolic consequence of the chronic intermittent hypoxia and sleep fragmentation caused by OSA?', '["Hypothyroidism", "Insulin resistance and an increased risk of developing Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus", "Hyperthyroidism", "Addison''s disease", "Increased bone density"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sympathetic surges and inflammatory cytokines released due to repeated hypoxic events severely impair glucose metabolism, independently of obesity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What happens to pulmonary artery pressure during a prolonged obstructive apneic event?', '["It drops to zero", "It remains completely stable", "Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causes acute spikes in pulmonary artery pressure, which can lead to chronic pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain", "It decreases slightly", "It causes massive pulmonary hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The lungs naturally constrict blood vessels in areas of low oxygen. In an apnea, the entire lung is hypoxic, causing global vasoconstriction and forcing the right heart to pump against immense resistance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male with untreated severe OSA frequently wakes up with a severe headache. What is the pathophysiological cause of these "morning headaches"?', '["Dehydration", "Cerebral vasodilation caused by nighttime hypercapnia (retained CO2 during apneas)", "Cerebral vasoconstriction caused by hypoxia", "A brain tumor", "Sinusitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CO2 is a potent cerebral vasodilator. The accumulated CO2 from hundreds of apneas causes the brain''s blood vessels to swell, increasing intracranial pressure and causing a throbbing morning headache that typically resolves within an hour of waking up and breathing normally.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of the epiglottis in the pathophysiology of OSA in some patients?', '["It is never involved", "It can flop backward (retroflexion) into the glottis during inspiration, acting like a trapdoor and causing severe, CPAP-resistant airway obstruction", "It shrinks during sleep", "It grows into a tumor", "It paralyzes the vocal cords"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A "floppy epiglottis" is a recognized cause of OSA. Because it acts like a one-way valve, applying CPAP can sometimes push it down harder over the airway, making the obstruction worse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with Acromegaly has a high risk of developing severe OSA. What is the primary anatomical reason?', '["Their vocal cords are paralyzed", "Excess growth hormone causes macroglossia (massive tongue enlargement) and generalized soft tissue hypertrophy in the pharynx", "Their neck bones fuse", "They develop central sleep apnea", "They lose all their teeth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tissue hypertrophy caused by acromegaly severely crowds the upper airway. Macroglossia is a classic finding.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is Hypothyroidism associated with an increased risk of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["It causes hyperventilation", "It leads to weight gain and the deposition of mucopolysaccharides (myxedema) in the tissues of the upper airway, making them bulky and floppy", "It shrinks the trachea", "It causes vocal cord paralysis", "It causes acid reflux"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Myxedema physically thickens the tongue and pharyngeal walls, while the associated weight gain further narrows the airway. OSA can often improve significantly just by correcting the thyroid hormone levels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Rostral Fluid Shift" and how does it contribute to OSA in patients with heart failure or end-stage renal disease?', '["Fluid shifting from the lungs to the brain", "During the day, fluid accumulates in the legs (edema). When the patient lies down to sleep, gravity shifts this fluid back up into the neck, drastically increasing tissue pressure around the pharynx and worsening collapsibility", "Fluid shifting from the stomach to the esophagus", "Fluid in the inner ear", "Tears pooling in the eyes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a major pathophysiologic mechanism in non-obese patients with fluid retention states. Diuretics or wearing compression stockings during the day can actually improve their sleep apnea at night.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which dimension of the pharyngeal airway is typically the most compromised in a patient with classic Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["The vertical length", "The anterior-posterior (AP) dimension, due to the tongue and soft palate falling backward", "The lateral (side-to-side) dimension only", "The subglottic diameter", "The tracheal diameter"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While lateral narrowing occurs, the primary mechanism of obstruction is usually the anterior structures (soft palate, tongue base, epiglottis) collapsing backward against the posterior pharyngeal wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does advancing age contribute to the worsening of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["The airway cartilages turn to bone", "There is a progressive loss of protective pharyngeal muscle tone and increased deposition of parapharyngeal fat, making the airway floppier", "The tongue stops growing", "The tonsils massively enlarge after age 60", "The brain stops responding to oxygen completely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Like most muscles, the pharyngeal dilators lose tone and speed of contraction with age, failing to effectively stent the airway open during sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with "Complex Sleep Apnea" (Treatment-Emergent Central Sleep Apnea). What does this mean?', '["They have both a brain tumor and a neck tumor", "They have Obstructive Sleep Apnea, but when they are placed on CPAP to open the airway, they begin having frequent Central apneas (brain stops initiating breaths)", "They refuse to wear their CPAP", "They have OSA complicated by asthma", "They snore without having apneas"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When the obstructive component is fixed by CPAP, the underlying instability in their central respiratory drive is unmasked. They often require a more advanced machine, like an ASV (Adaptive Servo-Ventilator), which breathes for them during central pauses.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Arousal Threshold" in the context of sleep apnea?', '["The volume of the alarm clock needed to wake the patient", "The level of negative intrathoracic pressure or hypoxia required to trigger the brain to wake up and restore muscle tone to the airway", "The pressure setting on the CPAP machine", "The amount of coffee required to stay awake", "The blood pressure during sleep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A low arousal threshold means the patient wakes up very easily, leading to highly fragmented sleep even with minor airway resistance. A high arousal threshold means the patient tolerates severe hypoxia before finally waking up, which is dangerous for the heart and brain.')
